<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Rag.NET.VectorStores.AzureAISearch</RootNamespace>
    <PackageId>Rag.NET.VectorStores.AzureAISearch</PackageId>
    <Description>Azure AI Search vector store for Rag.NET</Description>
    <!-- unit, not container, deliberately: the Docker-tier suite runs against a community
         azure-ai-search-simulator, not the real service. It implements no OData filter
         expressions (the filtered-search test is permanently skipped) and nothing has ever
         confirmed it behaves like real Azure AI Search. A simulator of unverified fidelity
         is a fake with a port number. -->
    <!-- Milestone 6, Phase 6.1: 13 tests against ghcr.io/ellerbach/azure-ai-search-simulator via
         Testcontainers, in the Docker tier. Phase 4.0 recorded the judgment that this is `unit`
         despite having Docker-tier tests, "its container is a community simulator without OData
         filters and of unconfirmed fidelity". 6.1 reverses the level and keeps the caveat, because
         the two are separable: the mechanism IS a container, and pretending otherwise hides a real
         suite; the fidelity gap is a property of that container and belongs in a reason, where it
         is machine-readable, rather than in a level that says nothing about it. -->
    <VerifiedBy>container</VerifiedBy>
    <VerifiedByReason>Exercised against a THIRD-PARTY simulator (ghcr.io/ellerbach/azure-ai-search-simulator), not Microsoft's service and not a vendor emulator — weaker than the vendor-published containers the other stores use. Unverified as a result: OData filter expressions, which the simulator does not implement (one test is skipped outright for this, and it is Milestone 4's recorded coverage gap), and any behaviour where the simulator's fidelity to the real service is unconfirmed — the suite already asserts that its hybrid fusion arithmetic is NOT the real service's.</VerifiedByReason>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleToAttribute">
      <_Parameter1>Rag.NET.VectorStores.AzureAISearch.Tests</_Parameter1>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- The "Rag.NET"-named ActivitySource core also links; see src/Shared/RagTelemetrySource.cs.
         Linked, not referenced: a ProjectReference to core would pull the whole pipeline into a
         vector store. -->
    <Compile Include="..\Shared\RagTelemetrySource.cs" Link="Telemetry\RagTelemetrySource.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Rag.NET.Abstractions\Rag.NET.Abstractions.csproj" />
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Search.Documents" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- One definition, linked rather than published, exactly as RagTelemetrySource is: the gate
         is an implementation detail of every vector store and belongs on no package's surface. -->
    <Compile Include="..\Shared\VectorStoreInitialisationGate.cs" Link="Internal\VectorStoreInitialisationGate.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
